            <ArticleTitle>Intravenous fluorescein as a cause of immunoglobulin E-mediated anaphylactic shock.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1066-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1399-6576.2012.02732.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a patient with severe anaphylactic shock immediately after injection of i.v. fluorescein. The patient recovered without sequela. Immunoglobulin E (IgE) mechanism was highly suggestive with significant increase in serum tryptase, positive basophil allergen threshold sensitivity (CD-sens) and histamine release tests towards fluorescein. This is, to our knowledge, the first report where CD-sens has been used to aid in diagnosing an IgE-mediated anaphylactic shock caused by fluorescein.</AbstractText>
